**(a) Importance of the operating system to a user (2 marks)**

The operating system (OS) is the software that makes the computer usable. For the user it:

- provides a user interface (graphical or command line) so that the user can give commands without knowing how the hardware works;
- loads and runs application programs, and lets several of them run at the same time (multitasking);
- manages the hardware resources for the user (processor, memory, disks, keyboard, screen, printers) and the files (create, save, copy, delete, organise in folders);
- protects the user's data and programs (user accounts, passwords, file permissions) and reports errors.

**(b) Why process management is necessary (2 marks)**

Many processes (programs in execution) exist at the same time, but the computer has only one CPU (or a few cores). The OS must therefore:

- decide which process uses the CPU, when and for how long (scheduling), so that the CPU is never idle and every process gets a fair share and makes progress;
- create, suspend, resume and terminate processes, and save and restore their state at each context switch;
- stop processes from interfering with each other, and let them communicate and synchronise (and avoid deadlock) when they share resources.

Without process management, one process could monopolise the CPU or processes could corrupt each other's work.
